Experiment: Cisco Packet Tracer – Network Design and Simulation

Aim
1. To understand the Cisco Packet Tracer environment and design a simple computer network.
2. To analyze the behavior of network devices using the Cisco Packet Tracer simulator.
3. To create networks using a hub, a switch, and both devices, and compare their functioning.
4. To identify and draw the network topology implemented in the college.

Requirements
- Cisco Packet Tracer software
- 4–6 PCs
- One hub
- One or two switches
- Copper straight-through cables

Algorithm / Procedure
Experiment 1: Network Using Hub
1. Open Cisco Packet Tracer.
2. Drag and place 4–6 PCs and one hub from the device library.
3. Connect each PC to the hub using copper straight-through cables.
4. Assign IP addresses to all PCs in the same subnet.
5. Example: PC0 – 192.168.1.1, PC1 – 192.168.1.2, PC2 – 192.168.1.3, PC3 – 192.168.1.4.
6. Click each PC, open Desktop → IP Configuration, and enter the IP address and subnet mask 255.255.255.0.
7. Use Add Simple PDU (envelope icon) to send a packet between two PCs.
8. Switch to Simulation mode and observe how the hub forwards the frame to all connected ports.
9. Record the observations.
